1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,888
Pick up exactly where the last lecture left off: the checklist agent built, wired

2
00:00:04,888 --> 00:00:06,905
and proven once against pinned data.

3
00:00:06,905 --> 00:00:11,498
What has not happened yet is a real week watching it run against rows nobody chose

4
00:00:11,498 --> 00:00:12,563
in advance for you.

5
00:00:12,563 --> 00:00:17,101
That is this lecture: a proper trial, the guardrails worksheet answered honestly,

6
00:00:17,101 --> 00:00:20,574
and the number this agent would actually cost to keep running.

7
00:00:21,124 --> 00:00:25,796
Open the C06 template the same way you did back in Section 7.2, and fill it in

8
00:00:25,796 --> 00:00:30,827
before a single real run happens: the same discipline as every trial in this course.

9
00:00:30,827 --> 00:00:35,619
Test data was realistic, not invented: real column values Priya's own colleagues

10
00:00:35,619 --> 00:00:37,416
actually type into that sheet.

11
00:00:37,416 --> 00:00:42,447
The expected answer for each row was worked out by hand first, so the agent's answer

12
00:00:42,447 --> 00:00:45,801
was something to check, not something to trust on sight.

13
00:00:45,801 --> 00:00:50,533
And the run count was written down before the trial started: five mornings, one

14
00:00:50,533 --> 00:00:54,127
working week, extended only if a miss ever went unexplained.

15
00:00:54,127 --> 00:00:59,098
The one question C06 asks that overrides the checklist itself, what would this cost

16
00:00:59,098 --> 00:01:01,314
unreviewed, got an honest answer too.

17
00:01:01,314 --> 00:01:06,226
A missed document surfacing only at renewal is an awkward phone call, not a lapsed

18
00:01:06,226 --> 00:01:11,137
policy, because Agent 4's chaser and Priya's own weekly report both still catch it

19
00:01:11,137 --> 00:01:12,515
from a different angle.

20
00:01:12,515 --> 00:01:17,486
That answer is what let a one-week trial feel like enough, rather than something to

21
00:01:17,486 --> 00:01:19,523
extend indefinitely out of nerves.

22
00:01:20,073 --> 00:01:21,684
Monday's run was the easy one.

23
00:01:21,684 --> 00:01:25,927
Oakfield had drifted inside the fourteen-day window over the weekend, all three

24
00:01:25,927 --> 00:01:29,311
documents still blank, and the agent flagged all three by name.

25
00:01:29,311 --> 00:01:33,179
Priya checked it against the sheet herself and moved on inside a minute.

26
00:01:33,179 --> 00:01:35,649
Wednesday told her something she already knew.

27
00:01:35,649 --> 00:01:40,054
Brightwell's signed form had arrived by post that morning, someone had updated the

28
00:01:40,054 --> 00:01:42,686
cell, and the agent correctly called it complete.

29
00:01:42,686 --> 00:01:47,198
Two clean passes in a row is exactly the kind of run that tempts you to stop reading

30
00:01:47,198 --> 00:01:50,528
closely, which is precisely why Section 7.1 warned against it.

31
00:01:50,528 --> 00:01:52,945
Friday is the run worth actually stopping on.

32
00:01:52,945 --> 00:01:57,457
Marlowe's fire safety certificate cell no longer read blank, it read "Sent, awaiting

33
00:01:57,457 --> 00:02:01,754
review," because a colleague in compliance had logged that the client's copy had

34
00:02:01,754 --> 00:02:05,192
arrived but had not yet been checked against the policy wording.

35
00:02:05,192 --> 00:02:09,542
So why would not blank versus not blank catch this, the same check you just built

36
00:02:09,542 --> 00:02:10,617
into your own agent?

37
00:02:10,617 --> 00:02:15,075
Because that phrase is not blank, and the system message never asked whether a cell

38
00:02:15,075 --> 00:02:18,351
said something specific, only whether it said nothing at all.

39
00:02:18,351 --> 00:02:22,057
Anything non-blank read as received, so the agent called it complete.

40
00:02:22,057 --> 00:02:26,408
Priya knew otherwise, because she had spoken to compliance that same morning, and

41
00:02:26,408 --> 00:02:29,309
that is the only reason this one did not slip through.

42
00:02:29,859 --> 00:02:34,536
Priya's fix was small, the same size as the fix Section 7.3 taught you to expect.

43
00:02:34,536 --> 00:02:39,271
She added one sentence to the system message: only count a document as received if

44
00:02:39,271 --> 00:02:43,948
the cell says exactly Received, and treat anything else, including a hopeful note

45
00:02:43,948 --> 00:02:46,893
like "Sent, awaiting review," as still outstanding.

46
00:02:46,893 --> 00:02:49,145
Notice what you do not see happen here.

47
00:02:49,145 --> 00:02:53,649
Nobody rewrote the agent, nobody added a second model to check the first one's

48
00:02:53,649 --> 00:02:54,168
homework.

49
00:02:54,168 --> 00:02:58,788
One person, reading every output during a trial that was still actually running,

50
00:02:58,788 --> 00:03:03,407
caught a gap between what a cell not being blank means and what a document being

51
00:03:03,407 --> 00:03:04,793
received actually means.

52
00:03:04,793 --> 00:03:09,297
That gap is easy for you to miss in the abstract and obvious the moment a real

53
00:03:09,297 --> 00:03:12,242
compliance note lands in a real column of your own.

54
00:03:12,792 --> 00:03:17,119
Fill this in honestly and sometimes the honest answer is that nothing changed.

55
00:03:17,119 --> 00:03:21,225
This build did not need a new credential, a new permission, or a new human

56
00:03:21,225 --> 00:03:25,774
checkpoint, because every piece of it was already something Agent 3 had earned the

57
00:03:25,774 --> 00:03:26,606
right to touch.

58
00:03:26,606 --> 00:03:30,933
Priya still opened the Google account's own consent screen and confirmed that,

59
00:03:30,933 --> 00:03:34,872
rather than trusting her memory of what she had granted months earlier.

60
00:03:34,872 --> 00:03:39,477
That single check is the reason you run the worksheet even when you are fairly sure

61
00:03:39,477 --> 00:03:44,082
of the answer already: Section 8.3 asks the question by name instead of letting you

62
00:03:44,082 --> 00:03:46,301
assume a scope stays put once it is set.

63
00:03:46,851 --> 00:03:51,581
That estimate is exactly what it says it is: a plausible worked number for a fifth

64
00:03:51,581 --> 00:03:56,370
agent inside a course, not a figure pulled off a real invoice, because there is not

65
00:03:56,370 --> 00:03:58,447
a real invoice behind Priya's story.

66
00:03:58,447 --> 00:04:01,158
The habit is the point, not the specific total.

67
00:04:01,158 --> 00:04:05,946
Count the task it replaces, count the time you now spend checking instead of doing,

68
00:04:05,946 --> 00:04:10,562
subtract one from the other, and write down what is left even when the number is

69
00:04:10,562 --> 00:04:10,966
modest.

70
00:04:10,966 --> 00:04:15,465
A number like that survives a follow-up question, which is the entire standard

71
00:04:15,465 --> 00:04:20,312
Section 9.2 set: not the biggest figure defensible, the smallest one nobody can poke

72
00:04:20,312 --> 00:04:20,888
a hole in.

73
00:04:20,888 --> 00:04:25,504
Say the sentence Section 10.1 taught you to say: this agent saves roughly thirty

74
00:04:25,504 --> 00:04:30,119
five minutes a week chasing paperwork by hand, costs nothing beyond what you are

75
00:04:30,119 --> 00:04:34,907
already spending on the other four, and never marks a document received on its own,

76
00:04:34,907 --> 00:04:38,657
so every flag still gets a human look before renewal day arrives.

77
00:04:39,207 --> 00:04:43,614
This build went faster than Agent 1 did back in Section 3, and it should have.

78
00:04:43,614 --> 00:04:48,304
Every decision inside it, the shape of the trigger, the wording of the ceiling, the

79
00:04:48,304 --> 00:04:52,937
two-line system message, was already a decision made once before somewhere earlier

80
00:04:52,937 --> 00:04:53,785
in this course.

81
00:04:53,785 --> 00:04:58,418
What is different by the fifth agent is not the difficulty, it is the amount of it

82
00:04:58,418 --> 00:05:01,074
you no longer have to think about from scratch.

83
00:05:01,074 --> 00:05:04,520
What is worth noticing is what did not get faster: the trial.

84
00:05:04,520 --> 00:05:09,266
Reading Friday's output carefully took exactly as much attention as reading the very

85
00:05:09,266 --> 00:05:13,843
first one back in Section 3.5 did, and it should, because that is the one step in

86
00:05:13,843 --> 00:05:16,781
this method that speed is never allowed to shortcut.

87
00:05:16,781 --> 00:05:21,527
That is the actual lesson of watching this run twice: the mechanics get quicker, the

88
00:05:21,527 --> 00:05:22,657
checking never does.
